import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/QrDemo
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Demo
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Generated/Sources

## The qr package: installing it, a live code that follows what is typed, its props, a code anywhere
## else (SVG for the server, the terminal), how big codes get, how it is made and tested, and what
## it doesn't do yet. The demo is the site's (site/Demos/QrDemo.rocx), hydrated.
meta = {
	title: "QR codes",
	description: "The qr package for Roc Solid apps: QR codes made in plain Roc, a <QrCode> component that draws one as SVG and follows its text, and SVG or terminal text for anywhere else.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing" },
		{ id: "demo", label: "A code that follows" },
		{ id: "props", label: "Its props" },
		{ id: "anywhere", label: "Anywhere else" },
		{ id: "sizes", label: "How big" },
		{ id: "made", label: "How it is made" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/qr</code>]}
				title="QR codes, in plain Roc"
			>
				Text in, a code out, drawn as SVG. A page shows one with a component that follows its text; the server
				draws one into an email; rocx prints one in the terminal.

			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use it when a phone should open something from a screen: a link to join a room, a ticket, a page's
				address. The encoder is plain Roc on no platform, with no JavaScript library or service to call, so
				the browser, the server, rocx and a test all run the same code.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in the app's header:</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc's header"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\", qr: \"../packages/qr/main.roc\" }"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				In a <code>.rocx</code> file, <code>import qr.QrCode</code> for the component and
				<code>import qr.Qr</code> for the encoder and its drawing functions. <code>rocx generate</code>
				(which <code>rocx dev</code> and <code>rocx build</code> run first) copies the component from the
				package's <code>components/</code> into the app, as
				<code>Generated/qr/QrCode.roc</code> (as for the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_charts}>charts</a>): a
				package can't import the platform's <code>Html</code> for the server and the browser at once.
			</p>

			<Heading id="demo">A code that follows</Heading>
			<p>
				Type some text and pick how much damage the code must survive. The line under the buttons says what
				it came to:
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo>
			<div data-demo="qr"><QrDemo /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<Code title={"${Sources.qr_use.file}, in part"} source={Sources.qr_use.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The demo keeps the text and level in two <a href={"${Paths.components}#primitives"}>signals</a>;
				<code>{"<QrCode>"}</code> encodes again whenever either changes.
			</p>

			<Heading id="props">Its props</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>text</code></td><td>What the code says: usually a URL. Like every value prop of an effectful component, it is a <a href={"${Paths.components}#passing"}>getter</a>, so the code follows it as it changes.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>level</code></td><td>How much damage the code survives: <code>Low</code> (about 7%), <code>Medium</code> (15%, the default), <code>Quartile</code> (25%) or <code>High</code> (30%). The higher the level, the bigger the code for the same text.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>label</code></td><td>What the code is, for screen readers. <code>"QR code"</code> by default.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>class</code></td><td>More classes for the <code>{"<svg>"}</code>, besides <code>qr-code</code>.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The component draws a square <code>{"<svg>"}</code> that fills its box's width, so give the box a
				width. It is black on white, with the four-module quiet zone readers need. Its colours are CSS
				(<code>.qr-light</code> and <code>.qr-dark</code>); keep them dark on light, as most readers can read
				nothing else.
			</p>
			<p>
				Text too long for any code at the chosen level draws an empty code, labelled
				<code>"…: the text is too long for a QR code"</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="anywhere">Anywhere else</Heading>
			<p>
				Outside a page, call the encoder: <code>Qr.encode(text, level)</code> returns the code, or
				<code>Err(TooLong)</code>. A code is a record: <code>size</code>, its side in modules, and
				<code>dark</code>, its modules row by row (<code>Bool.True</code> for dark). Draw it with:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>Qr.svg(code)</code>: a whole SVG document, for an image in an email or a file</li>
				<li><code>Qr.path(code)</code> and <code>Qr.view_box(code)</code>: every dark module as one SVG path, and the box around it, for markup of your own (this is what the component draws)</li>
				<li><code>Qr.terminal(code)</code>: text for a terminal, two rows of modules per line, black on white whatever the terminal's colours</li>
				<li><code>Qr.pbm(code)</code>: a PBM image, one pixel per module</li>
				<li><code>Qr.is_dark(code, x, y)</code>: one module; <code>Qr.capacity(level)</code>: the most bytes a code holds at that level</li>
			</ul>
			<Code
				title="A code for an email"
				source={"match Qr.encode(\"https://example.com/tickets/17\", Medium) {\n\tOk(code) => Qr.svg(code)\n\tErr(TooLong) => \"\"\n}"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				rocx uses the terminal drawing: <code>rocx dev --android --lan</code> prints a code for a phone on the
				same Wi-Fi to scan, and <code>{"rocx qr <text>"}</code> prints any text's code.
			</p>

			<Heading id="sizes">How big</Heading>
			<p>
				QR codes come in 40 sizes, called versions: version 1 is 21 modules a side, and each adds 4, up to 177
				at version 40. The package picks the smallest version that holds the text's UTF-8 bytes at your
				level. The most each level holds:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Survives</th><th>Version 1 holds</th><th>Version 40 holds</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Low</code></td><td>about 7% damaged</td><td>17 bytes</td><td>2,953 bytes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Medium</code></td><td>15%</td><td>14 bytes</td><td>2,331 bytes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Quartile</code></td><td>25%</td><td>11 bytes</td><td>1,663 bytes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>High</code></td><td>30%</td><td>7 bytes</td><td>1,273 bytes</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A link to a page fits a small code: a 60-byte URL at <code>Medium</code> is version 4, 33 modules a side.
			</p>

			<Heading id="made">How it is made</Heading>
			<p>
				The encoder follows the standard (ISO/IEC 18004), step by step as Project Nayuki's reference encoder
				takes them:
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>The text's bytes are encoded in byte mode, with the smallest version that holds them.</li>
				<li>Reed–Solomon error correction is added, in the blocks the standard sets for each version and level.</li>
				<li>The finder, timing and alignment patterns are placed, and version information from version 7 on.</li>
				<li>Each of the eight masks is tried, and the one the standard's penalty rules score lowest is kept.</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				<code>roc test packages/qr/main.roc</code> checks the standard's numbers: format and version bits,
				capacities, where alignment patterns go, and the error-correction generator.
				<code>packages/qr/test/decode.sh</code> has a real reader (zbar) read back a code at every version and
				level, filled to capacity: all 160 read back as their text.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Byte mode is the only mode. Digits alone, or upper-case letters and digits, would fit in smaller codes
					in the standard's numeric and alphanumeric modes.
				</li>
				<li>
					The level is never raised for you, even when the chosen version could hold the text at a higher one.
				</li>
				<li>No logo in the middle, and no Kanji mode, ECI, structured append or micro QR codes.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Each is sketched in the package's <code>PROPOSALS.md</code>.</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
